.TH HEAD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
head \- output the first part of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B head
.RB [ \-c
.RI [ \- ] num
|
.B \-n
.RI [ \- ] num ]
.RB [ \-qvz ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B head
.BI \- num\c
.RB [ bkmcl ][ qv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B head
copies the first 10 lines of each
.I file
to standard output.
With no
.IR file ,
or when
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
standard input is read.
.PP
When more than one
.I file
is given, the output for each is preceded by a header of the form
.PP
.EX
==> \fIname\fR <==
.EE
.PP
with a blank line before every header except the first.
Standard input is shown as
.BR "standard input" .
.PP
A count given with a leading
.B \-
(for example
.BR "\-n \-5" )
selects everything
.I except
the last
.I num
lines or bytes.
For a seekable file this is done in two passes; for a pipe or other
non-seekable input the whole input is held in memory first.
.SS Number suffixes
The
.I num
argument of
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
may carry a case-insensitive suffix:
.BR K " (1024), " M " (1024^2), " G ", " T ", " P ", or " E
(successive powers of 1024), optionally followed by
.B i
and/or
.BR B ,
so
.BR KiB ,
.BR kB ,
and
.B K
all mean 1024.
A bare trailing
.B B
multiplies by 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n " [\fB\-\fR]\fInum\fR, " \-\-lines= [\fB\-\fR]\fInum\fR
Output the first
.I num
lines (default 10), or with a leading
.BR \- ,
all but the last
.I num
lines.
.TP
.BR \-c " [\fB\-\fR]\fInum\fR, " \-\-bytes= [\fB\-\fR]\fInum\fR
Output the first
.I num
bytes, or with a leading
.BR \- ,
all but the last
.I num
bytes.
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
may not be combined; if either is repeated, the last one wins.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-\-silent
Never print headers.
Takes precedence over
.B \-v
regardless of order.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Always print headers, even for a single input.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
Lines are delimited by NUL bytes instead of newlines.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a help summary to standard output and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing.
.SS Obsolete syntax
An argument of the form
.BI \- num
followed by optional letters is accepted anywhere before
.BR \-\- .
The letters are:
.TP
.B b
Bytes, multiplied by 512.
.TP
.B k
Bytes, multiplied by 1024.
.TP
.B m
Bytes, multiplied by 1048576.
.TP
.B c
Bytes.
.TP
.B l
Lines (the default).
.TP
.BR q ", " v
As
.B \-q
and
.BR \-v .
.PP
For example,
.B head \-20 file
prints 20 lines and
.B head \-2kq a b
prints the first 2048 bytes of each file without headers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All inputs were processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid options or counts, or at least one
.I file
could not be opened, read, or written.
Remaining files are still processed after a per-file error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the first 5 lines of two files:
.PP
.EX
$ head \-n 5 a.txt b.txt
.EE
.PP
Print everything except the last 2 KiB of a file:
.PP
.EX
$ head \-c \-2K image.bin > trimmed.bin
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR pr (1),
.BR tail (1)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
options with non-negative counts follow POSIX.1-2024.
Negative counts and the long options follow GNU coreutils; the
number suffixes follow the BSD
.B expand_number
convention.
.SH BUGS
When headers are enabled, the header for a file that cannot be
opened is printed before the error message.
